/**
\page RCD Residual Current Devices (RCDs)

This page introduces the key concepts behind **Residual Current Devices (RCDs)** — sometimes referred to as
**Residual Current Circuit Breakers (RCCBs)** or **Residual Current Devices with Over‑current protection (RCBOs)**.

The purpose of this material is to establish a **conceptual and system‑level understanding** of RCD operation,
measurement techniques, and regulatory requirements.  
Armed with these concepts, the reader should be able to explore detailed standards and product implementations independently.

<hr>

\section RCDPrinciple Principle of Operation

A Residual Current Device protects against **electric shock and fire hazards** by detecting **imbalance between conductors** supplying a load.

Under normal operating conditions:
\f[
    I_\mathrm{L} = I_\mathrm{N}
\f]

Where:
- \f$ I_\mathrm{L} \f$ is the line (phase) current
- \f$ I_\mathrm{N} \f$ is the neutral current

If some current leaks to earth (e.g. through a human body or faulty insulation):
\f[
    I_\Delta = | I_\mathrm{L} - I_\mathrm{N} |
\f]

When this **residual current** exceeds a predefined threshold (e.g. 30 mA),
the RCD disconnects the supply within a specified time.

<hr>

\section DifferenceCurrentSensing Sensing Current Difference

Residual current detection is fundamentally based on **measuring the vector sum of all live conductors** supplying a load.

This is typically achieved using a **core‑balance current transformer (CBCT)**.

\subsection CoreBalanceCT Core‑Balance Current Transformer

A core‑balance CT consists of a magnetic core through which **all live conductors** (L, N, and optional phases)
pass together.

- Under normal operation, the magnetic flux cancels:
\f[
    \sum I = 0
\f]

- Under fault conditions, a net flux is produced:
\f[
    \sum I \neq 0
\f]

This net flux induces a current in the secondary winding proportional to the **residual current**.

\subsection SignalConditioning Signal Conditioning

The output of the residual current transformer must be conditioned before it can be processed
by analog comparators or ADC subsystems.

Typical signal conditioning stages include:

- Burden resistor (current‑to‑voltage conversion)
- Rectification or absolute‑value detection
- Filtering to suppress high‑frequency noise
- Gain scaling to match ADC/comparator input range

\warning
Filter time constants directly impact **trip time** and must be carefully selected to
meet regulatory disconnection requirements.

<hr>

\section RogowskiRCD Rogowski-Based Residual Current Sensing

In addition to traditional core‑balance current transformers, **Rogowski‑based sensing**
is increasingly used in modern RCD designs, particularly where **wide bandwidth**,
**DC immunity**, or **compact mechanical integration** is required.

When paired with a suitable analog front end (AFE) such as the **RAA788000**
and a **flux concentrator**, Rogowski sensing can be adapted effectively for
residual current detection.

\subsection RogowskiPrinciple Rogowski Principle Applied to RCDs

A Rogowski coil is an **air‑cored, toroidal winding** that produces a voltage
proportional to the **time derivative of the enclosed current**:

\f[
    V_\mathrm{coil}(t) = K \frac{dI(t)}{dt}
\f]

Where:
- \f$ I(t) \f$ is the residual current waveform
- \f$ K \f$ is the mutual inductance constant of the coil

Unlike current transformers:
- There is **no magnetic core saturation**
- Sensitivity is **independent of absolute line current**
- Response extends to **very low and very high frequencies**

For RCD applications, the Rogowski coil is arranged such that **all live conductors**
pass through the sensing aperture, or flux concentrator, measuring only the **residual current**.

\remark
Because a Rogowski coil responds to \f$ dI/dt \f$, signal integration is required
to reconstruct the residual current magnitude.

\subsection FluxConcentrator Flux Concentrator

A key challenge in Rogowski‑based RCD sensing is achieving sufficient sensitivity
at **low residual current levels** (e.g. 10–30 mA).

A **flux concentrator** addresses this by:
- Enhancing the magnetic field coupling into the Rogowski coil
- Improving signal‑to‑noise ratio at low currents
- Reducing dependency on precise conductor positioning

\warning
Flux concentrators must be carefully designed to avoid introducing
**hysteresis**, **thermal drift**, or **directional sensitivity** that could
impact trip consistency.

\subsection RAA788000AFE Rogowski AFE Using RAA788000

The **[RAA788000](https://www.renesas.com/en/products/raa788000)** is the first of its kind coil-on-chip rogowski IC with integrated op-amps.

Signal chain:

1. Rogowski coil output (dI/dt signal) - provided by RAA788000
2. Low‑noise amplification & filtering - provided by RAA788000
3. Post processing (MCU + ADC/Comparator)

Key functional attributes include:
- Low input‑referred noise to support low \f$ I_\Delta \f$
- Stable integration over temperature and time
- Predictable phase response for time‑based trip evaluation

\remark
Using an integrated AFE reduces component tolerance errors and simplifies
compliance with trip‑time accuracy requirements.

<hr>

\section TripLogic Trip Logic and Decision Making

Once a residual current signal is obtained, the decision logic determines **when to trip**.

Two common implementation approaches are used:

\subsection AnalogTrip Analog Trip

- Comparator detects residual current above threshold
- Directly drives trip solenoid or latch
- Simple, fast, and robust

\subsection DigitalTrip Digital Trip

- Residual signal sampled by ADC
- Digital filtering and integration applied
- Software determines trip condition
- Enables:
  - Multiple trip curves
  - Self‑test
  - Event logging
  - Adaptation to different RCD types

\remark
Digital RCD implementations must carefully manage **latency and failure modes** to ensure safety equivalence
with analog designs.

<hr>

\section RCDTypes RCD Types

Standards classify RCD behaviour according to the **residual current waveform** they can detect.

| **Type** | **Detects** | **Typical Applications** |
|---------|-------------|--------------------------|
| **Type AC** | Sinusoidal AC only | Legacy residential systems |
| **Type A** | AC + pulsating DC | Appliances with rectifiers |
| **Type F** | AC + pulsating DC + mixed-frequency | Variable‑speed drives |
| **Type B** | AC + DC + smooth DC | EV charging, PV inverters |

<hr>

\section Compliance Compliance

RCDs are **safety‑critical devices** and are subject to stringent regulatory requirements.
Compliance ensures correct operation under fault conditions, environmental stress,
and electromagnetic disturbance.

\warning
This section provides reference information only.
Formal compliance requires consultation with notified bodies and accredited test facilities.

\subsection IECStandards Global Standards – IEC

The **International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC)** defines the foundational standards for RCDs:

| **Standard** | **Description** |
|---------------|----------------|
| **IEC 61008** | General requirements for RCDs without over‑current protection (RCCB - Residual Current Circuit Breaker) |
| **IEC 61009** | General requirements for RCDs with over‑current protection (RCBO- Residual Current Breaker + Overcurrent) |
| **IEC 60947‑2** | Industrial circuit breakers with residual current protection |
| **IEC 60755** | General safety requirements & testing methods for residual current devices |
| **IEC 62423** | Safety, performance & testing requirements for Type F and Type B RCD requirements |

<hr>

\subsection RegionalStandards Regional / Bloc Standards (EU & UK)

In the EU and UK, IEC standards are adopted through **EN standards**
and enforced via national regulations.

Typical compliance hierarchy:

| **Step** | **Entity** | **Purpose** |
|-----------|-------------|-------------|
| 1 | **Requirements** (Low Voltage Directive / UK Electrical Safety Regulations) | Legal safety requirements |
| 2 | **Standards** (EN 61008, EN 61009, EN 62423) | Technical compliance definition |
| 3 | **Type Testing** | Functional and safety verification |
| 4 | **Certification** | Issuance by notified body |
| 5 | **Declaration of Conformity** | Manufacturer declaration |

<hr>

\subsection TripCharacteristics Trip Characteristics

RCD standards define:
- Rated residual current \f$ I_{\Delta n} \f$
- Maximum disconnection time as a function of \f$ I_\Delta \f$

Typical values:

| **IΔn** | **Maximum Trip Time** |
|--------|------------------------|
| 1 × IΔn | ≤ 300 ms |
| 2 × IΔn | ≤ 150 ms |
| 5 × IΔn | ≤ 40 ms |

<hr>

\subsection UsefulReferences Useful References

- **IEC:** https://www.iec.ch
- **UK Electrical Safety:** https://www.legislation.gov.uk  

<hr>
*/